t $C$ has a $y$-coordinate of $-2$, so we move $2$ units *down*, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/49/49069b4b9be29c0a999e7d2b71eec9a973eda9ad1f8a2a7b897d76970771a644.png)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186054597 1074 "Starting at the origin $O,$ we move along the $x$ and $y$ axes to get to the point $C.$\n\nFirst, we move along the $x$-axis. The point $C$ has an $x$-coordinate of $1,$ so we move $1$ unit to the *right*, in the *positive* direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nThen, we move along the $y$-axis. The point $C$ has a $y$-coordinate of $-2,$ so we move $2$ units *down*, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/49/49069b4b9be29c0a999e7d2b71eec9a973eda9ad1f8a2a7b897d76970771a644.png)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054598 1074 "To find the coordinates of $D$, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point $D$ is $3$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-3$.\n\nAdditionally, the point $D$ is $2$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, $D$ has a $y$-coordinate of $-2$.\n\nThe point $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-3$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-2$, so its coordinates are $(-3,-2)$." 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186054598 1074 "To find the coordinates of $D,$ we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point $D$ is $3$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-3.$\n\nAdditionally, the point $D$ is $2$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, $D$ has a $y$-coordinate of $-2.$\n\nThe point $D$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-3$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-2,$ so its coordinates are $(-3,-2).$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054599 1074 "In order to identify the quadrant to which the point $(4,-2)$ belongs, we start by plotting the point.\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of the point is $4$, so we move $4$ units to the right of the origin.\n- The $y$-coordinate of the point is $-2$, so we move $2$ units down from the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02b0dd5d2d125277ffb6fc637fa612eef451c06522efa7655d8374c68641e796.png)\n\nBased on the graph, we can tell that the point belongs to quadrant $\\textrm{IV}$, the fourth quadrant." 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186054599 1074 "In order to identify the quadrant to which the point $(4,-2)$ belongs, we start by plotting the point.\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of the point is $4,$ so we move $4$ units to the right of the origin.\n\n- The $y$-coordinate of the point is $-2,$ so we move $2$ units down from the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02b0dd5d2d125277ffb6fc637fa612eef451c06522efa7655d8374c68641e796.png)\n\nBased on the graph, we can tell that the point belongs to quadrant $\\textrm{IV}$,  the fourth quadrant." 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426965 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426966 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426971 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426978 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426968 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426970 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426973 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426976 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426977 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426969 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426972 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426979 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426980 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426967 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426974 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426975 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426981 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186054933 1050 420.0 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055120 1071 "Which of the following shows the point $C(-2,-3)$?" 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186055120 1071 "Which of the following shows the point $C(-2,-3)?$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055120 1074 "Starting at the origin, we move along the $x$ and $y$ axes to get to the point $C.$\n\nFirst, we move along the $x$-axis. The point $C$ has an $x$-coordinate of $-2,$ so we move $2$ units to the *left*, in the negative direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nThen, we move along the $y$-axis. The point $C$ has a $y$-coordinate of $-3,$ so we move $3$ units *down*, in the negative direction of the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b3ac34b4fe6c877d606d8ddde5f29c9856cc4bdc5ac14e3ffcf5ddccdc0ebb.png)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055278 1074 "We label the quadrants as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d1e7a9315f6b0a938fdc502f370c90f29d5bbeaa063fc27a7df81360f01adaf.png)\n\nSince $A$ is placed below and to the right of the origin, it lies in quadrant $\\text{IV},$ which is the fourth quadrant." 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055439 1071 "![](images/df/df0d0dc8ce2cbc15cf6b2060f1a402f876c984cc884d70508cc6a55850e28ed3.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-coordinate of the point $R$?" 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186055439 1071 "![](images/df/df0d0dc8ce2cbc15cf6b2060f1a402f876c984cc884d70508cc6a55850e28ed3.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-coordinate of the point $R?$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055439 1074 "To find the coordinates of $R,$ we start at the origin, move $3$ units *left*, and then $3$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/3e/3e2476fece6e8789c5560a6c16242efa5da955e39317be06823416f9ae9c8661.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $R$ are $(-3,-3).$ Therefore, the $x$-coordinate of $R$ is $-3.$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055942 1064 "The **Cartesian coordinate system** (or **Cartesian plane**) is a grid that we can use to understand where certain objects lie, similar to a map. This grid looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fd/fd702a53ff1127a2432cbdd9a3c7057a08c73ac7a086454c066fa2c96bea7974.png)\n\nIt consists of a horizontal number line called the **$x$-axis** and a vertical number line called the **$y$-axis**.\n\nThe **origin** is the location where the $x$ and $y$ axes meet and is given the symbol $O$." 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186055942 1064 "The **Cartesian coordinate system** (or **Cartesian plane**) is a grid that we can use to understand where certain objects lie, similar to a map. This grid looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fd/fd702a53ff1127a2432cbdd9a3c7057a08c73ac7a086454c066fa2c96bea7974.png)\n\nIt consists of a horizontal number line called the **$x$-axis** and a vertical number line called the **$y$-axis**.\n\nThe **origin** is the location where the $x$ and $y$ axes meet and is given the symbol $O.$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055943 1064 "Every location on the Cartesian plane is called a **point**. Every point is identified using an $x$**-coordinate** and a $y$**-coordinate**. Together, points are expressed using an **ordered pair** of **coordinates** $(x,y)$.\n\nFor example, we can mark (or **plot**) the point $A$ with coordinates $({\\color{red}{3}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ by starting from $O$ and then traveling $\\color{red}3$ units towards the right, followed $\\color{blue}2$ units up.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ef61dedbbcc6eedcd125c2c62cdfbaa4d9b9a12f10181714b51c060a059fd3.png)\n\nWe say that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $3$,\n- the $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $2$,\n- the $xy$-coordinates (or just \"the coordinates\") of $A$ are $(3,2)$.\n\nThe letter $A$ is just a label for our point. It's usually a good idea to label points!" 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186055943 1064 "Every location on the Cartesian plane is called a **point**. Every point is identified using an $x$**-coordinate** and a $y$**-coordinate**. Together, points are expressed using an **ordered pair** of **coordinates** $(x,y).$\n\nFor example, we can mark (or **plot**) the point $A$ with coordinates $({\\color{red}{3}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ by starting from $O$ and then traveling $\\color{red}3$ units towards the right, followed $\\color{blue}2$ units up.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ef61dedbbcc6eedcd125c2c62cdfbaa4d9b9a12f10181714b51c060a059fd3.png)\n\nWe say that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $3$,\n\n- the $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $2$,\n\n- the $xy$-coordinates (or just \"the coordinates\") of $A$ are $(3,2).$\n\nThe letter $A$ is just a label for our point. It's usually a good idea to label points!" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055957 1064 "Remember that a coordinate plane is made by two number lines that meet at $0$. Since these are number lines, we can extend both to include negative numbers.\n\n![](images/f1/f1eeb7f3bc0ab8b39e9dc7127d76a32f1dd130d380748fedc6e11b967f3878ed.png)\n\nWe plot an ordered pair $({\\color{red}x},{\\color{blue}y})$ similar to before, but if the number is negative, we reverse the direction. So, starting at the origin:\n\n- if the first number is positive, move that number of places *right* along the $\\color{red}x$-axis. If its negative, move *left*.\n- if the second number is positive, move that number of places *up* along the $\\color{blue}y$-axis. If its negative, move *down*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the coordinates of the point $Q$ above.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $Q$, we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}3$ units *left*, and then $\\color{blue}2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/b4/b4f58f0c1ad6dc2936ed1b24c844fa82c1b1d5ec10c80e14c6a43f6f4eaf5544.png)\n\nSince we move left, the $\\color{red}x$-coordinate is negative, and since we move down, the $\\color{blue}y$-coordinate is negative.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $({\\color{red}{-3}},{\\color{blue}{-2}})$." 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186055957 1064 "Remember that a coordinate plane is made by two number lines that meet at $0.$ Since these are number lines, we can extend both to include negative numbers.\n\n![](images/f1/f1eeb7f3bc0ab8b39e9dc7127d76a32f1dd130d380748fedc6e11b967f3878ed.png)\n\nWe plot an ordered pair $({\\color{red}x},{\\color{blue}y})$ similar to before, but if the number is negative, we reverse the direction. So, starting at the origin:\n\n- if the first number is positive, move that number of places *right* along the $\\color{red}x$-axis. If its negative, move *left*.\n\n- if the second number is positive, move that number of places *up* along the $\\color{blue}y$-axis. If its negative, move *down*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the coordinates of the point $Q$ above.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $Q,$ we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}3$ units *left*, and then $\\color{blue}2$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/b4/b4f58f0c1ad6dc2936ed1b24c844fa82c1b1d5ec10c80e14c6a43f6f4eaf5544.png)\n\nSince we move left, the $\\color{red}x$-coordinate is negative, and since we move down, the $\\color{blue}y$-coordinate is negative.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $({\\color{red}{-3}},{\\color{blue}{-2}}).$" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055958 1064 "The axes split the coordinate plane into four parts, called **quadrants**.\n\n![](images/93/938125e855fcb96f743c01f2a00d289ef13e0601772bab56cf0f255e9adf4dcd.png)\n\nGoing counter-clockwise, it's common to call\n\n- the top-right quadrant the first quadrant (I),\n\n- the top-left is the second quadrant (II),\n\n- the bottom-left is the third quadrant (III),\n\n- and and bottom-right is the fourth quadrant (IV).\n\nTo remember the order of the quadrants, it's helpful to think about writing the letter \"C\" in the graph:\n\n![](images/1e/1ef91f2bc25ea8b887ec449d2dafe2eaf6a157733e7bc50e4480280df8190728.png)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186055958 1064 "The axes split the coordinate plane into four parts, called **quadrants**.\n\n![](images/93/938125e855fcb96f743c01f2a00d289ef13e0601772bab56cf0f255e9adf4dcd.png)\n\nGoing counter-clockwise, it's common to call\n\n- the top-right quadrant the first quadrant (I),\n- the top-left is the second quadrant (II),\n- the bottom-left is the third quadrant (III),\n- the and bottom-right is the fourth quadrant (IV).\n\nTo remember the order of the quadrants, it's helpful to think about writing the letter \"C\" in the graph:\n\n![](images/1e/1ef91f2bc25ea8b887ec449d2dafe2eaf6a157733e7bc50e4480280df8190728.png)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 false] [17592186426863 1080 "2" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426863 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426863 1083 #uuid "0001803c-afd3-592d-b6a0-b411a5d09ba9" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426864 1080 "3" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426864 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426864 1083 #uuid "000de45b-de2f-5f3f-acd3-854e731ec44c" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426865 1080 "(-2,-4)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426865 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426865 1083 #uuid "011074f1-cfd6-58bf-a370-02a373de9fcb" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426866 1080 "(3,-2)" 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426866 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534593 17592186045417 true] [17592186426866 1083 #uuid "06388a3b-d728-5519-b802-